@@ -213,9 +213,10 @@ How long a signed-in Matrix session lasts is set on the **Configuration** page o
 - **What it is.** An ABSOLUTE ceiling from the sign-in (`matrix_sessions.session_expires_at` of an OAuth session; the `expires_at` bound of a legacy login): a refresh never extends it, this story adds no sliding window. It applies to the OAuth and to the legacy login (`Nebu.Web.SessionConfig.matrix_session_ttl_seconds/0`). The admin session ceiling (`NEBU_ADMIN_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS`) is not part of it.
 - **Existing OAuth sessions keep their ceiling.** A change applies to the NEXT sign-in; OAuth sessions issued before keep the `session_expires_at` they were issued with, also across refreshes. To end them earlier use the revoke paths of the activation checklist.
 - **Legacy sessions follow the setting (accepted).** A legacy (non-OAuth) session has no stored ceiling: the bound of its extension by the background refresh worker and of the refresh-token reuse window is `created_at` plus the CURRENT lifetime. A lowered value therefore stops the extension of existing legacy sessions earlier, a raised value lets the worker extend them up to the new bound; their `expires_at` is never shortened. The absolute guarantee above is for OAuth sessions only.
-- **Unreadable configuration.** If `server_config` cannot be read, a login uses the environment value / 8 hours (fail safe; the failed read is not cached, the next call reads again). A saved value that is damaged or out of range is ignored (never clamped) with one `WARNING` that names the key and never the value.
+- **Unreadable configuration: last-known-good.** A saved lifetime stricter than the environment value / 8 hours must never turn longer because a read failed. Each node therefore keeps the last successfully read valid value, without a TTL; if `server_config` cannot be read, or the saved row is damaged or out of range (ignored, never clamped), the last-known-good value is used. The environment value / 8 hours apply only when no good value was ever loaded on this node, or after a successful read that found no row (you removed it on purpose); a save on this node replaces the value at once. The failed read itself is not cached, the next call reads again. The `WARNING` for a damaged row is limited to one per minute per node and names the key, never the value.
 - **Protection and audit.** The same as the OAuth switch: admin role, CSRF, strict fetch metadata and a fresh sign-in (marker bound to `/admin/config`; the form shows "Sign in again to save" until you have signed in, then "Save session lifetime"). One `audit_log` row `session_lifetime_changed` per change (actor, `before_seconds`, `after_seconds`: numbers only), written in the same transaction as the value; if the row cannot be written nothing is saved (a shorter lifetime is not exempt). A value outside the bounds is refused with a message next to the field and nothing is saved or audited.
-- **Effect.** The saving node uses the new value at once; every other node within `NEBU_OAUTH_CONFIG_CACHE_MS` (default 2 s, at most 5 s), because the value is read through the same per-node cache as the OAuth switch (no `server_config` query per login).
+- **Effect and cache.** The saving node drops its cache entry and uses the new value at once, except for a read that was already in flight and can refill the cache with the old value for at most one TTL (the in-flight refill race); every other node follows within `NEBU_OAUTH_CONFIG_CACHE_MS` (default 2 s, at most 5 s), because the value is read through the same per-node cache as the OAuth switch (one `server_config` query per TTL window, not per login). With the cache switched off (`NEBU_OAUTH_CONFIG_CACHE_MS=0`) every login, `/refresh` and worker row costs one query per call.
+- **Legacy login and the identity provider.** A legacy login is bounded by `min(exp of the IdP token, now + lifetime)`: raising the setting does not lengthen a legacy login beyond the expiry (`exp`) of the identity provider's own token; the OAuth login ceiling is not capped by it.
 - **Migration 000056** adds `matrix_session_ttl_seconds` to the `config_update_mutable` row-level-security allowlist of `server_config`. Without it the first save works and the second fails with an RLS error. Rolling-deploy safe: old nodes never read the key.
 
